data.content:
This is the **C# 14 span overload resolution** breaking change.

## What happened

In C# 14, new built-in conversions from arrays to `Span<T>`/`ReadOnlySpan<T>` mean that `array.Contains(num)` now resolves to **`MemoryExtensions.Contains`** (a span-based method) instead of **`Enumerable.Contains`**. 

`MemoryExtensions.Contains` is **not representable in LINQ expression trees**, so the expression tree compiler throws at runtime.

## How to fix

Force the call back to `Enumerable.Contains` by casting the array to `IEnumerable<T>` or using `.AsEnumerable()`:

```csharp
// Option 1: .AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 2: cast to IEnumerable<int>
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);

// Option 3: explicit static call
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

All three prevent the compiler from choosing the span overload, keeping the call within what expression trees support.
